<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "blog", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %> &middot; 8 November 2026</p>
  <h1>skyBlip, SoftRF and Stratux: why start again?</h1>
  <p class="lede">Pilots who know the open-source projects ask us two things first. Is this a modified version of SoftRF? And what does it do that a Stratux with a T-Beam does not? Both projects have been around longer and offer many functions. Here is what we owe them, and what we set out to do differently.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">What we owe</h2>
  <h3>Ten years of open-source work, then a fresh start.</h3>
  <p>skyBlip is not a modified version of another project. We learned a great deal from SoftRF, from the OGN Tracker and from open-source radio libraries. Their authors were working on these subjects long before us. We then wrote the firmware from scratch, closer to the hardware, to gain speed and battery life.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">What we aimed for</h2>
  <h3>Three goals the other projects did not set themselves.</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ready without a workshop.</strong> Not every pilot has the time or the skill to assemble a Stratux, and its parts cost more. You can buy the hardware for a Go fully assembled from LilyGO or on Amazon, and <%= link_to_page "install", label: "Briefing installs skyBlip from a browser" %>, with no soldering, no command line and no account.</li>
    <li><strong>A day on battery.</strong> No cable and no power socket to plan in the cockpit. On the bench, a parked Go ran 19&nbsp;hours on one charge as part of a Tango, linked to a Bravo over Bluetooth with its GPS updating twice a second. A parked Go+ ran 49&nbsp;hours on one charge. That is why we chose a low-power processor and an e-paper screen that stays readable in full sun.</li>
    <li><strong>Conformity you can check.</strong> skyBlip has held its own manufacturer address range in the ADS-L registry since October 2026. Every clause of the specification implemented by the firmware has its own conformance test. Every release passes these tests. The documentation is generated from the same tests. We are preparing the <%= link_to_page "ads-l/conformity", label: "declaration of conformity" %>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">What it does not do</h2>
  <h3>A Stratux receives more systems. A Go receives fewer, but fits in your pocket.</h3>
  <p>A Stratux receives ADS-B, UAT, OGN and more, and sends it all to your tablet over Wi-Fi in GDL&nbsp;90. If you have one and it suits you, keep it.</p>
  <p>We chose a board with Bluetooth but no Wi-Fi, because Wi-Fi draws far more current. GDL&nbsp;90 travels over Wi-Fi, so Go cannot send it. It sends NMEA data over Bluetooth instead, to three devices at once. SDVFR Next, SkyDemon and ForeFlight read this format.</p>
  <p>A Go receives ADS-L and FLARM. For ADS-B, it pairs with a second pocket device, <%= link_to_page "skyblip-bravo", label: "Bravo" %>, rather than growing into a bigger box.</p>
</section>

